---

<Base
  title="Architecture — Tuile"
  description="The Tuile workspace: a render-agnostic core, strict dependency rules, the graphics budget, and the GeometryStream trait every viewer, film and renderer consumes."
  kicker="Architecture"
  heading="One core, strict seams, replaceable everything else."
  lede="The whole design is an argument about where the dependencies are allowed to point. The core holds the standard and depends on nothing; every renderer, transport, cache and host integration hangs off a named trait."
>
  <h2>The workspace</h2>

  <p>
    A Cargo workspace of small crates with one shared dependency table, so the graph builds
    exactly one copy of the maths type, one error type, one runtime. Thirty-one crates are
    on <code>main</code> at v0.14.0; one more is <span class="tag review">in review</span>.
  </p>

  {crates.map((g) => (
    <>
      <h3>{g.group}</h3>
      <ul class="crate-list">
        {g.items.map(([name, what, state]) => (
          <li>
            <span class="name">{name}</span>
            <span class="what">
              {what} {state === 'review' && <span class="tag review">in review</span>}
            </span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  ))}

  <h3>Connectors</h3>
  <p>
    Two further crates connect to specific commercial terrain and imagery services. They are
    named after the service they talk to, which is the single deliberate exception to the
    naming rule; they are ordinary <code>TileFetcher</code> and source implementations, and
    the core has no idea they exist. That is the purity test for the seam: adding a whole
    data provider changes nothing inside <code>tuile-core</code>. A token-free path exists
    beside them: any quantized-mesh <code>layer.json</code>, any XYZ imagery template, and
    a heightfield of your own served over plain HTTP.
  </p>

  <h3>Outside the crates</h3>
  <p>
    <code>packages/tuile-viewer</code> is the ES module a web page imports: the viewer in
    WebAssembly, its geometry server in a Web Worker. <code>examples/</code> holds the
    desktop viewer binary, the heightfield server, a catalog proxy that keeps a connector's
    token off the client, and headless harnesses for bulk frames, cache warming and probing
    what a traversal selects. <code>integrations/</code> holds the C++ Hydra plugin and the
    images and job scripts around it. GPU texture compression lives in a sibling repository,
    <a href="https://github.com/lapoule-dev/texcomp" rel="noopener">texcomp</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Dependency rules</h2>

  <p>
    The arrows only ever point one way. This is checked by reading, by CI, and by the
    WebAssembly target, which fails loudly the moment something native sneaks into the core.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{rules}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The core is runtime-agnostic on purpose: it exposes async traits and pure data, and the
    caller supplies the execution. That is what lets the same traversal run inside a native
    window, inside a browser worker, inside a film bake and inside an offline renderer's
    plugin.
  </p>

  <h2>The foundational traits</h2>

  <pre><code>{traits}</code></pre>

  <p class="note">
    On single-threaded WebAssembly the <code>Send</code> bound of the async traits is
    dropped for that target rather than being wished away. Preparing GPU resources is the
    backend's own business: it reports what it holds through the protocol instead.
  </p>

  <h2>The geometry server is a trait, not a server</h2>

  <p>
    The executable heart of the project is a <strong>logical</strong> geometry server:
    traversal, the graphics budget, fetching, decoding. It is not a network server.
    Streaming is defined at the protocol level, in the core, and transports are merely
    implementations of it. The server decides; the consumer executes — it uploads what was
    planned, in order, within its frame's deadline, and acknowledges each upload with the
    bytes it really holds.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{stream}</code></pre>

  <h3>Bindings, one protocol</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Binding</th><th>What it is</th><th>Status</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>InProcessStream</code></td>
        <td>The geometry server runs inside the consumer's own process; messages pass over channels with zero serialisation. Desktop and phone viewers, the film bake, the Hydra plugin.</td>
        <td><span class="tag shipping">shipped</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Worker binding</td>
        <td>In a browser, the geometry server runs in a Web Worker; each message crosses <code>postMessage</code> as one transferred buffer and is read back into the same types.</td>
        <td><span class="tag shipping">shipped</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Static HTTP pull</td>
        <td>No remote geometry server: the traversal runs on the consumer side and pulls static files over HTTP. This is how every Tuile viewer reads its sources today.</td>
        <td><span class="tag shipping">shipped</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>WsStream</code></td>
        <td>A remote process runs the geometry server per session for a consumer that declares its budget, and pushes geometry as glb.</td>
        <td><span class="tag planned">planned</span></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A renderer written against <code>GeometryStream</code> works over each of them without
    a line changed. Where the geometry server runs is decided by its budget's inputs: on an
    interactive device it runs locally, because memory pressure must act at once and a head
    rotation cannot wait a round trip.
  </p>

  <h2>Data flow</h2>

  <pre><code>{flow}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Traversal and budget are pure functions of their inputs — tree, views, residency,
    arrivals, platform signals — with no I/O and no clock inside. That is the property that
    makes them testable, portable and replayable: the same inputs give the same decisions,
    bit for bit, and CI simulates the budget on every change.
  </p>

  <h2>Compression is the client's choice</h2>

  <p>
    Imagery can be held on the GPU as BC7 or ASTC 4×4 blocks — a quarter of the memory of
    RGBA8. Whether it is, is decided by the application that uses Tuile, never by Tuile: it
    is off by default, and off is byte-identical to the path without compression. A client
    that opts in describes its GPU with plain capability flags — no graphics-API type
    reaches the core — and gets a plan: encode on the GPU at upload, encode on the CPU in a
    browser's decode workers, or transcode imagery stored as KTX2. Anything that cannot be
    encoded is uploaded as RGBA8, so the ground is never left without a texture. Films stay
    RGBA8.
  </p>

  <h2>Embedding: a frame the host owns</h2>

  <p>
    A game engine owns its device, its colour target and its depth buffer, and has usually
    drawn into them already. <code>tuile-wgpu</code>'s <code>HostPass</code> takes those
    three and the frame's encoder, records its pass, and submits, presents and clears
    nothing it was not asked to — any sample count, forward or reverse-Z, with or without
    a stencil. The viewer's own frames go through the same pass. At the application level,
    <code>tuile-viewer</code> is generic over where terrain and imagery come from, and lets
    a host place the camera each frame and read the ground beneath it.
  </p>

  <h2>Geospatial precision</h2>

  <p>
    3D Tiles coordinates are Earth-centred, Earth-fixed metres: around 6.4 million of them.
    A 32-bit float resolves about half a metre at that magnitude, which is guaranteed visual
    jitter. The protocol is fixed and absolute:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Every upstream computation — bounding volumes, composed transforms, screen-space error — in f64.</li>
    <li>Choose a local origin, at the root volume's centre or re-anchored near the camera.</li>
    <li>Subtract in f64, <em>then</em> cast to f32 for the GPU.</li>
    <li>Build the view matrix relative to that same origin.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The network middleware</h2>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>Status, plainly.</strong> The network server — a socket binding of the
    protocol, and a static server for tilesets — is specified in the repository's design
    documents, and the protocol it would carry lives in <code>tuile-core</code>, but
    <strong>no such server is in the workspace</strong>. What serves today is narrower: the
    source-tile store and its read API, used by the film pipeline; a heightfield served as
    terrain and imagery; and a proxy that keeps a connector's token off the client. The
    <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a> says where it sits.
  </p>

  <p>
    When it lands, the shape is already decided: one service written once against the
    storage and cache traits, with the HTTP framework and the edge runtime as thin adapters
    over it, rather than two forks of the same routes. Static responses carry a
    content-hash entity tag and immutable cache headers, because a tile is immutable by
    construction. The design rule for that mode is short — the server does nothing a content
    delivery network could do, except when there is no content delivery network.
  </p>

  <p class="next">Next → <a href="/3d-tiles">What 3D Tiles actually is</a></p>
</Base>
